WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:04.907
Ukraine’s railroad has become a lifeline
for refugees.

00:00:04.907 --> 00:00:07.792
All these stations are gateways to safety.

00:00:07.792 --> 00:00:12.748
That lead to Lviv, which has become a vital way
out of the country.

00:00:12.748 --> 00:00:15.150
From here, most travel to Przemyśl.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:18.922
A small Polish town 8 miles from 
the Ukrainian border.

00:00:18.922 --> 00:00:22.373
Its train station has transformed 
into a relief center.

00:00:22.373 --> 00:00:25.349
Inside, volunteers welcome the refugees

00:00:25.349 --> 00:00:29.617
and offer them food as they figure out 
where to go next.

00:00:29.617 --> 00:00:32.689
Many have brought their pets on the long journey.

00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:35.802
But they've only brought as much as they can carry.

00:00:35.802 --> 00:00:37.680
Like Maryna and her son

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:42.448
who spent the night here at the train station
after leaving everything behind.

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:55.309
Daria spent six hours waiting in an underpass
on her journey here.

00:01:00.399 --> 00:01:05.894
Sisters Martina and Sabina were asleep in their homes 
when their city came under fire.

00:01:18.426 --> 00:01:21.685
As Russia continues to move further into Ukraine,

00:01:21.685 --> 00:01:25.815
this critical web of escape routes is 
increasingly under attack.

00:01:25.815 --> 00:01:30.722
Making it harder and harder for millions of
Ukrainians to escape the invasion.

00:01:36.736 --> 00:01:39.716
Since Russia invaded Ukraine on February 24th

00:01:39.716 --> 00:01:43.569
more than 3 million people 
have fled their homes.

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:45.600
Nearly all of them are moving west

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:50.000
away from Russia and towards 
these countries over here.

00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:52.880
And the vast majority, around 2 million

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:56.018
are going here, 
to Poland.

00:01:56.018 --> 00:01:58.785
Most of them are women and children.

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:02.885
Ukraine is requiring most men 
between the ages of 18 and 60

00:02:02.885 --> 00:02:05.970
to stay behind and encouraging them to fight.

00:02:25.885 --> 00:02:29.920
Since the war began, Ukraine has closed its
airspace to civilian flights.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.953
And the roads have become increasingly dangerous.

00:02:32.953 --> 00:02:37.994
Railway stations are the most important points
in the country so far 

00:02:37.994 --> 00:02:42.529
because people can get on the train on the cities 

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:46.108
and move to west and save their lives.

00:02:46.108 --> 00:02:47.818
That’s Oleksandr Kamyshin.

00:02:47.818 --> 00:02:50.670
He is in charge of a mobile command team 
that has evacuated

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:52.987
more than 2 million people by train.

00:02:52.987 --> 00:02:57.618
We focus on different cities, 
depending on the bombing.

00:02:57.618 --> 00:03:03.357
Once new cities are under shelling, 
we increase the capacity for the cities.

00:03:03.357 --> 00:03:07.607
Things change so fast, Ukrainian Railways
releases a daily schedule.

00:03:07.607 --> 00:03:14.772
And so far, the most popular routes are 
Kyiv-Lviv, Kharkiv-Lviv, Dnipro-Lviv.

00:03:14.772 --> 00:03:18.576
At the stations, people are packed into 
evacuation trains 

00:03:18.576 --> 00:03:22.206
and it’s in large part because of Oleksandr's policy.

00:03:22.206 --> 00:03:26.480
All people who would like to leave the city
can do it within one day.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.405
So we finish the day with zero people 
on the railway station.

00:03:30.405 --> 00:03:33.213
But it’s not just about getting on the train

00:03:33.213 --> 00:03:37.295
the journey itself is getting more dangerous too.

00:03:37.295 --> 00:03:41.605
On March 13th, Russian forces hit 
an evacuation train here

00:03:41.605 --> 00:03:44.768
killing the conductor and 
stranding hundreds.

00:03:44.768 --> 00:03:47.754
Further north, near Kharkiv, 
an undetonated bomb

00:03:47.754 --> 00:03:50.909
landed right by the train tracks 
a few days earlier.

00:03:50.909 --> 00:03:54.719
Ukrainian Railway workers had to defuse 
and remove it.

00:03:54.719 --> 00:03:58.308
Damaged tracks like these, can temporarily
stop escape routes.

00:03:58.308 --> 00:04:02.384
We got the greatest team of 
infrastructure professionals

00:04:02.384 --> 00:04:07.018
who start repairing the track 
once the bombing stops

00:04:07.018 --> 00:04:09.698
and they do it in hours, not days.

00:04:09.698 --> 00:04:13.849
But bombed bridges, like this one,
can shut them down permanently.

00:04:16.049 --> 00:04:19.480
The areas over here, where Russia is 
fighting for control

00:04:19.480 --> 00:04:22.034
are especially dangerous for trains to reach.

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:27.314
As a result, most of these stations shut down,
soon after the war began.

00:04:27.314 --> 00:04:28.852
Including Mykolaiv.

00:04:28.852 --> 00:04:31.823
The port city Martina and Sabina fled.

00:04:37.669 --> 00:04:41.674
Their parents drove them to Odesa, where
the train station was still operating

00:04:41.674 --> 00:04:44.102
so they could make it to Lviv.

00:04:50.162 --> 00:04:53.049
Poland has become the top destination 
for refugees 

00:04:53.049 --> 00:04:56.149
because it has a very long border with Ukraine.

00:04:56.149 --> 00:04:59.169
It’s also culturally and linguistically similar

00:04:59.169 --> 00:05:03.985
and already has over a million Ukrainians
living and working in the country.

00:05:03.985 --> 00:05:07.979
But it’s also because soon after 
Russia invaded Ukraine

00:05:07.979 --> 00:05:10.990
the EU did something unprecedented.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:13.166
“Europe will be there for them

00:05:13.166 --> 00:05:18.321
not only in the first days but also in the
weeks and months to come."

00:05:18.321 --> 00:05:22.889
They agreed to activate this directive, which
provides temporary protection.

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:25.470
It was first issued in 2001

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.289
after a decade of wars in Southeastern Europe

00:05:28.289 --> 00:05:30.740
displaced millions of people.

00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:35.532
The directive allowed for temporary protection
for people fleeing from non-EU countries.

00:05:35.532 --> 00:05:38.002
But it was never invoked.

00:05:38.002 --> 00:05:39.312
Until now.

00:05:39.312 --> 00:05:41.759
What the Temporary Protection Directive offers

00:05:41.759 --> 00:05:45.893
is a range of rights that are applicable immediately.

00:05:45.893 --> 00:05:48.137
And these include residency rights

00:05:48.137 --> 00:05:52.159
right to have access to the labor market,
right to healthcare,

00:05:52.159 --> 00:05:55.442
social protection, but also right to education.

00:05:55.442 --> 00:06:01.031
What is also notable is that this mechanism
was adopted by all the EU member states.

00:06:01.031 --> 00:06:04.240
This means that the millions of people 
fleeing Ukraine

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:09.020
can continue on to any of the 27 EU countries
with these protections

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:10.912
for up to three years.

00:06:10.912 --> 00:06:17.309
This is a testimony to the large scale of
the crisis that we're seeing now in Europe.

00:06:17.309 --> 00:06:20.120
But despite this directive, not all seeking refuge

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.708
 have been treated the same way on the ground.

00:06:22.708 --> 00:06:26.509
Many non-Ukranians have experienced racism
as they’ve fled.

00:06:26.509 --> 00:06:29.786
Reports include being pushed to the back of
the lines at the border

00:06:29.786 --> 00:06:32.409
and being turned away at hotels.

00:06:34.778 --> 00:06:38.404
In Przemyśl though, thousands keep arriving
every day.

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:56.807
For many refugees arriving in Przemyśl, this
is just the first stop out of Ukraine.

00:06:56.807 --> 00:07:01.117
Martina and Sabina have family in Poland 
to help them get started.

00:07:01.117 --> 00:07:04.259
But the vast majority will go on 
to reception centers

00:07:04.259 --> 00:07:09.293
or move deeper into Europe 
and farther away from home.

00:07:33.224 --> 00:07:35.301
Some people cry, some people smile.

00:07:35.301 --> 00:07:37.563
Some people are happy. Some people are not happy.

00:07:37.563 --> 00:07:41.340
But finally, all the people who left the cities,
which are shelled

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:45.885
they can sleep, at least in the calm place
and without the bombing.

00:07:45.885 --> 00:07:48.835
That's the most important thing 
we keep in mind.

00:07:48.835 --> 00:07:52.387
That gives me the understanding 
that we do right job.

